{"name":"Diamond Light","hex":"#dfe7ec","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"diamond","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A glimmering glass-like mineral that is an allotrope of carbon in which each atom is surrounded by four others in the form of a tetrahedron.","A gemstone made from this mineral."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of, or containing diamond, a diamond or diamonds.","Of, relating to, or being a sixtieth anniversary."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/diamond"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"}],"entries":[{"title":"Diamond","blocks":[{"text":"Diamond is a mineral form of the element carbon with its atoms arranged in a crystal structure called diamond cubic. Diamond is a tasteless, odorless, strong, brittle solid, a poor conductor of electricity, colorless in pure form, and insoluble in water. Another solid form of carbon known as graphite is the chemically stable form of carbon at room temperature and pressure, but diamond is metastable and converts to it at a negligible rate under those conditions. Diamond has the highest hardness and thermal conductivity of any natural material, properties that are used in major industrial applications such as cutting and polishing tools."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Rough_Diamond.jpg/330px-Rough_Diamond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond","wikidata":"Q5283"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Diamant_gisements.jpg/500px-Diamant_gisements.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Diamant_gisements.jpg/1280px-Diamant_gisements.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Diamant_gisements.jpg/500px-Diamant_gisements.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Main diamond producing countries (in French)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiamant_gisements.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Diamond_structure.gif/500px-Diamond_structure.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Diamond_structure.gif/500px-Diamond_structure.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Diamond unit cell, showing the tetrahedral structure","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiamond_structure.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Diamond_face_trigons_scale.jpg/500px-Diamond_face_trigons_scale.jpg?utm_source=en